From: ae-automation-bot <ae-automation-bot@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 18:54:22 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 285/333] [ae-patch] Keep queued notes through a page reload

A message sent while the chat is busy waits in the client-side queue
(queuedMessages) until the turn ends, and that queue lived only in page
memory. A reload, a phone tab discard, the chunk-error auto-reload or the
"Omnigent was updated" reload during a long turn lost it without a word; on
2026-11-01 a note went this way during a 28-minute turn.

New aeQueuePersist.ts mirrors the queue into sessionStorage
(omnigent:ae-queued-messages:v1) synchronously on every queuedMessages
change, storing every field but the File objects (their names are kept) and
a firstSeenAt stamped once per note. Temp conversations are not stored, and
past 50 notes new ones stay in memory only while the stored ones stay.

Once per page it puts the notes back. Storage is read before the write
subscription exists, and every write keeps those notes until the restore is
done, so a second reload during the restore cannot wipe them. A Web Lock
named after a per-tab id (kept in sessionStorage) decides ownership: a
reload waits up to a second for the dying page to free it; a duplicated tab
finds it held, takes a fresh id and leaves the notes to the tab that has
them, with a toast. Owned notes come back ahead of anything queued, keyed
q_ae_<stableId> with their stable id kept so the server append stays
idempotent. A fresh text-only note sends through the existing flush paths;
one with attachments, one over an hour old, one already waiting for Retry,
or any note in a browser without Web Locks comes back waiting for Retry,
with one toast saying why. A notes document written for another signed-in
user is dropped. QueueFlushProvider installs it, which covers the main app
and the embed entry.
